    redos-001: ? — ?
      
    lfi-005: anyio.open_file — logic_gap
      If the application allows setting the path parameter (e.g., via URL query 
or POST body), an attacker can use absolute paths like /etc/passwd or traversal 
sequences like ../../etc/passwd.
    lfi-006: anyio.open_file — logic_gap
      GET /file?path=/etc/passwd HTTP/1.1
    suspicious-008: get_path — logic_gap
      On Windows, an attacker can send a request with path like 
`/.../.../.../windows/win.ini` or use drive letters like 
`C:/.../windows/win.ini`. On Unix, if symlinks are allowed, an attacker can 
create a symlink pointing to `/etc/passwd` and access it via the static file 
URL.
    suspicious-009: get_response — logic_gap
      An attacker sends a request to `/static/../../../etc/passwd`. If 
`lookup_path` does not properly resolve or block `..`, the resulting `full_path`
will be `/etc/passwd`, and `FileResponse` will serve it. Alternatively, if 
symlinks are allowed, an attacker can create a symlink pointing to `/etc/passwd`
and access it.
    lfi-011: anyio.open_file — logic_gap
      Send a request with a crafted path like /../../../etc/passwd to read 
arbitrary files.
    suspicious-015: lookup_path — logic_gap
      Send a request to the static file endpoint with a path like 
`../../../etc/passwd`. The `lookup_path` function will join this with the base 
directory (e.g., `/static/`) to form `/static/../../../etc/passwd`, which 
resolves to `/etc/passwd`. If the file exists and is readable, it will be 
served.

